From 151f9c01a006812932ca6472e541901f6729611e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 13:20:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(moat): the accuracy record, and the engine as a metered
 machine interface
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Patch 0013. A competent engineer can rebuild the constraint physics from public
sources in a month. Everything shipped so far is therefore copyable. This patch
builds the two things that are not: a record of where this engine's predictions
landed against instrumented sites, which accrues one hall at a time and cannot be
bought or scraped, and a metered interface that sells the engine to software
rather than to people.

THE ACCURACY RECORD (gridforge/calibration/)

An observation is what the model said next to what the site turned out to be. The
ledger is worth nothing the moment it can be gamed, so the rules are in code, not
in intent:

  * E5 or above, or it is refused. Anything weaker is another run of our own
    model, and a ledger fed with model output validates the engine against itself
    — the most attractive lie available to this business.
  * Client names never enter it. site_ref() hashes site and hall. The aggregate is
    meant to be published, and an asset needing a confidentiality review before
    publication is worth far less than one that does not.
  * The committed ledger refuses client data outright, with no override flag. The
    one way this leaks is a hurried engineer reaching for a convenient default, so
    the convenient default is impossible. Same seed/local split as the cost
    library; calibration.local.json is gitignored.
  * One site-month counted once. A duplicate inflates n and understates spread.

States are uncalibrated (n=0), indicative (1-4: no spread is quoted, because a
range from four halls is fake precision) and calibrated (n>=5). Nothing smooths,
fits or extrapolates.

It prints zeros today. That is the point. Section 14 of every study says accuracy
has not been established, /v1/calibration says it in public, and the block rides
on every API response including the free one. A report that omits its accuracy
record when the record is empty has told the reader the question does not matter.
Nobody else prints this because nobody else survives printing it.

Keys are written out by hand rather than derived from the registry — renaming a
constraint must not silently orphan four years of observations — and a test
asserts the vocabulary still covers every constraint the engine evaluates.

THE MACHINE INTERFACE (/v1/tools, /mcp, /v1/usage, /developers)

The next customer is a portfolio tool, a diligence stack, an agent doing site
selection. GET /v1/tools publishes every callable in OpenAI function shape and MCP
shape from one definition; POST /mcp is Model Context Protocol over JSON-RPC 2.0,
implemented on the stdlib because an agent-facing surface is the last place to
start trusting transitive packages.

Metering bills units, not calls: a qualify and a forty-hall portfolio run do not
cost the same. Rates are published at /v1/version — a meter whose rate is secret
is a meter nobody integrates against. The free tier bills zero and never touches a
quota; a demo that can exhaust an allowance is a trap. Keys carry their plan
(sk_x:acme:5000), bare keys still authenticate, so billing lands on a running
deployment without breaking anyone.

Over quota returns 402, never 429. To a machine those are different instructions:
429 says retry, 402 says buy more. An agent told to retry a call it can never
afford will retry it forever and the customer finds out from the invoice.

The evidence discipline tightens rather than relaxes for machines. Every response
carries evidence classes, provenance digests and the calibration block; the MCP
handshake instructs the model not to present an E0 or E1 figure as a property of a
physical asset. A modelled number loose in an agent loop is more dangerous than
one in a board pack — nobody reads the footnote and nothing catches it downstream.

ALSO

  * gridforge calibrate show|add|keys|where, and gridforge tools [--json -o]
  * public/reference/tools.json and calibration.json are generated by the engine
    and covered by the existing drift guard, so /developers renders the schema the
    server actually validates against
  * load_ledger(published_only=True), so a build script cannot leak a local ledger
    into public/
  * an empty ledger file reads as empty rather than corrupt

TESTS

43 new (214 total). The rules are tested harder than the arithmetic: a weak
observation is refused, a client name cannot reach the ledger, the committed
ledger cannot be written, a duplicate is refused, no spread is quoted below n=5,
E6 cannot be claimed without observations, an empty ledger still prints
uncalibrated, 402 arrives instead of 429, the free tool leaks no priced content
through MCP, a corrupt meter file never costs a caller their answer, and the
accuracy section does not trip the report language gates.

tsc clean, next lint clean, next build clean with /developers and /reference in
the route table.

